<article class="env-remark" id="0AN7">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0AN7">Remark <span data-tag="0AN7">87.19.12</span> <span class="named">(Warning)</span>.</a>  The discussion in Lemmas <a href="/tag/0AN5" data-tag="0AN5">87.19.8</a>, <a href="/tag/0AKP" data-tag="0AKP">87.19.9</a>, and <a href="/tag/0AKQ" data-tag="0AKQ">87.19.10</a> is sharp in the following two senses: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $A$ and $B$ are weakly admissible rings and $\varphi : A \to B$ is a continuous map, then $\text{Spf}(\varphi ) : \text{Spf}(B) \to \text{Spf}(A)$ is in general not representable. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $f : Y \to X$ is a representable morphism of affine formal algebraic spaces and $X = \text{Spf}(A)$ is McQuillan, then it does not follow that $Y$ is McQuillan. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> An example for (1) is to take $A = k$ a field (with discrete topology) and $B = k[[t]]$ with the $t$-adic topology. An example for (2) is given in Examples, Section <a href="/tag/0ANY" data-tag="0ANY">110.77</a>. </p>
</article>